# Demand for Police Department Grants
Tamil title: நிதி ஒதுக்கீட்டுக் கோரிக்கை - காவல் துறை

Speech delivered in the Legislative Assembly on 18 March 1958.

Mr Speaker, before the allocation to the Police Department is granted, a cut motion gives the Opposition an opportunity to express its views. Debate upon the police grant naturally includes connected matters beyond the department itself.

Whether on the Opposition or ruling side, no one has the slightest doubt about the competence of our State police. It is efficiently administered by capable people. The disagreement concerns the purposes for which the rulers employ so capable a force. Saying that personnel work efficiently is one matter; asking what their efficiency serves is another.

One member suggested arrangements that would bring no discredit to the department. His words appeared to imply dissatisfaction with police competence. Our argument is not that officers lack ability but that the ruling party uses them improperly.

Politics has long possessed a dictionary according to which a ruling party must always defend every official action. Whichever party takes office uses that dictionary. The result is that, despite possessing an able police service, its good work for the public is obstructed.

Kari Gounder spoke enthusiastically of the department's good work. I listened closely. He has become accustomed to repeating, "I speak from the ruling party." Let that pass. I do not say the department fails in its work. The report shows only about 24,000 police serving a population of three crore and fifty-seven lakh. Comparing those numbers, I recognise the difficulty of their task. As was said here, police must walk a tightrope like circus performers.

We strongly condemn the ruling party's use of people who already work under such hardship. The Minister may ask for proof. Members of Government know that the Opposition has limited means to collect it.

Police Minister Bhaktavatsalam once said that during his Congress activity a police officer struck him three times, that he felt no pain then but did later, and that the officer subsequently rose to a high post. If police repression is used against us, it brings no disgrace upon the Opposition; it cultivates hostility toward the ruling party and gives its leaders a bad name. Nor does it weaken our cause.

Public disturbances cannot automatically be blamed upon one party. The rulers must examine why people agitate and employ methods other than a rain of repression.

In Britain, a country that praises its own maintenance of peace, law and order, a minister who advocated raising rents was pelted with eggs and tomatoes and prevented from speaking. Newspapers did not report tear gas, gunfire or a severe baton charge. They reported only that the minister was escorted away under protection. He reportedly told the crowd that although they disliked his proposal then, they might support him when they later judged it correct.

Cabinets may change frequently; the police service should not change with each ministry. Yesterday the Finance Minister said of the Balwantrai Mehta Committee that the Government could have concealed its report had it wished. That very mentality sometimes pushes the police down an improper path.

Some members say democratic rights have produced disorder. Rulers must treat the police department as a temporary responsibility entrusted to them, not permanent property. The police institution is permanent; a ministry is not. My strong charge is that a temporary ministry uses the permanent police to make its own political influence permanent. Ministers may not always do this personally, though in some cases I suspect even personal prompting.

My friends Anbazhagan and Sathyavani Muthu complained to the Police Minister about excesses in Madras and the disorderly conduct of officers in their constituencies. What action followed? When none follows, political figures publicly boast in market streets that no one can transfer the police officers, that if the DMK seeks transfers they will go to the Minister and prevent them. I need not identify the party. Such claims encourage some officers to behave improperly.

There is no need to question police ability. Their competence helps them protect so large a country. But the department exists to maintain law, order and peace. We must ask when those things break down and why. It is useless simply to say the Opposition breaks the law.

If laws command public respect, and if administrative order is such that even opponents recognise a duty to obey it, the police need not face today's continual pressures. Ministers ignore the first signs of unrest. When a strike is approaching, they fail to settle causes before agitation erupts. They then send the police to suppress people driven by emotion.

Electricity workers in the State announce a strike. Non-gazetted officers present demands. B and C Mills workers declare that they will act to relieve their distress. When police strike crowds, they cannot distinguish family and political relationships. In one actual case, the officer who struck a DMK participant was the participant's own elder brother. The rulers must accept responsibility for drawing police into such a wrong course.

When complaints are made, ruling-party members demand, "Can you show evidence?" I never expected retired Inspector-General Rajarathnam to be discussed again here. After Anbazhagan explained the matter, a Congress member said an inquiry had produced nothing. Yet the letter written by the very person appointed to conduct the inquiry remains with us. The OCR of the procedural distinction in this passage is damaged, but the larger point is clear: denial does not discharge the duty to investigate.

When Anbazhagan raised the complaint, the answer was merely "No, no, no." A responsible Government, not the Opposition, bears the duty to investigate complaints against officials. Opposition members cannot be expected to assemble an entire prosecution file. Even when Kalyanasundaram produced evidence upon evidence, what answer came except the familiar one that an inquiry had been held and later abandoned?

A Salem member praised police skill, and I welcome that. They have competently discovered fraud even in cooperative institutions. I ask only for what purposes the rulers employ that skill. I feel pity, not anger, for police placed in a difficult position by the ministry responsible for law and order.

The departmental report contains a case summary printed in English but rendered illegible by Tamil OCR. The following Tamil explanation says that, after prosecution and inquiry proved an MLA not guilty, the report still stated that an MLA had robbed Rs 166 from a shop. Was it included so the Minister could repeatedly enjoy reading, "An Opposition member stole Rs 166"? The report does not even contain a clear qualification such as "alleged." This reveals that the defective element lies in the rulers' attitude, not police competence.

Kari Gounder said emotionally that democracy granted rights of speech and agitation and that wrongdoing followed. He appears to speak from an old outlook. As long as the ruling party claims a democratic path, granting those rights is its foremost duty, not a favour it may advertise.

Indonesia shows what can occur when democracy is denied. You have not bestowed rights unknown elsewhere or beyond what the age itself demands. Yet the ruling party expects gratitude as though we were beggars picking up bread thrown from a master's table.

You desire an authoritarian path but feel ashamed to say so openly. Then say plainly that you deny these rights. Enact a law that Opposition parties may neither hold meetings nor write. Many countries show what follows. The Finance Minister may deceive himself by imagining that mentioning them proves Annadurai has foreign connections.

Freedom of speech and writing are ordinary rights in many countries. Britain is ruled by a Queen, yet its Communist Party can resolve that monarchy should be abolished and speak for that policy in a square opposite the palace. What democratic rights unavailable elsewhere have you uniquely given us? Such rights can produce good results.

When disorder occurs at meetings, police must respond with maturity. Reports say that elsewhere police use rubber bludgeons rather than hard batons, stunning rioters without causing grave injury. Some disperse crowds with water from fire-fighting equipment. In a cold country people may flee cold water. I do not say the same method should simply be copied here: in our heat, agitators might buy a one-anna cake of Sunlight soap, bathe and go home. But a method free of brutality can be found, allowing police to act effectively.

Police ability is gradually being reduced by present duties. When I travel, I see an officer standing beneath every tamarind tree, not for one day but many. Asked why, some say a national leader is arriving from one direction and a minister from another. If necessary, establish a separate protective unit for ministers and visiting dignitaries.

An able police force should detect murder, robbery and other crimes and enforce the law, not spend its strength guarding ministers and attending endless opening ceremonies. Such diversion leaves too little capacity for real police work.

In a democracy, police should handle Opposition agitation judiciously so that the rulers do not acquire a bad name. Send one or two senior officers abroad to study and receive training in how other countries manage these situations.

Members on both sides have described hardships faced by police personnel. I again ask that they be relieved from those conditions. I conclude by repeating the charge that the ruling party employs the Police Department in an improper manner.

---NOTES---
The printed OCR date “78.03.1958” is read as 18 March 1958, supported by the filename `nithi180358`; the Tamil source remains unchanged.

The population and police-strength figures are translated from OCR readings and should be checked against the scan before statistical citation.

The English case summary on image 9 was processed with Tamil OCR and is illegible. Its adjacent Tamil explanation establishes an acquittal and a disputed report of Rs 166; no missing English wording is invented.

The speech's references to Britain, Indonesia, monarchy, communism and crowd-control methods are historical comparative arguments, not current operational guidance.
